Board apps have a built-in LLM helper injected by the runtime. When writing a board app's JavaScript, you can call:

  const result = await board.llm(messages, options?)

Parameters:
  - messages: Array<{role: 'user'|'assistant'|'system', content: string}>
  - options (optional): { model?: string, system?: string, maxTokens?: number, temperature?: number }

Returns: Promise<{ ok: boolean, text: string, model: string, error?: string }>

This uses the server's configured LLM provider — no API keys needed in the board app.
Example:
  const res = await board.llm([{role:'user', content:'Summarize this data: ' + JSON.stringify(data)}]);
  document.getElementById('output').textContent = res.text;

The function is available globally on `window.board.llm` in any board app served by tianshu.

Board apps have a built-in Data Source helper for querying databases (Neo4j, MySQL, etc.):

  const result = await board.ds(source, query, params?)

Parameters:
  - source: string — data source name (from board.dsList())
  - query: string — SQL or Cypher query
  - params (optional): object — query parameters

Returns: Promise<{ columns: string[], rows: object[], rowCount: number, error?: string }>

List available data sources:
  const { connections } = await board.dsList()
  // connections: [{name, type, description}]

Example:
  const { rows } = await board.ds('helu-graph', 'MATCH (p:Product) RETURN p.title LIMIT 10');
  rows.forEach(r => console.log(r['p.title']));

Both board.ds() and board.dsList() are available globally on window.board.
